---
import type { OutlineEntry } from '../components/outline-entry';
import Scorecard from '../components/Scorecard.astro';
import type { SubjectResults } from '../components/subject-results';
import { cssResults } from '../config/css-results';
import type { DebatePart } from '../config/debate-part';
import { debateParts } from '../config/debate-parts';
import { htmlResults } from '../config/html-results';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { togetherResults } from '../config/together-results';
import PartLayout from '../layouts/PartLayout.astro';

const part: DebatePart | undefined = debateParts.find((candidate: DebatePart): boolean => candidate.path === '/Verdict');

if (part === undefined) {
    throw new Error('src/config/debate-parts.ts has no part at /Verdict.');
}

const outline: ReadonlyArray<OutlineEntry> = [
    { id: 'scorecard', text: 'The scorecard' },
    { id: 'choose', text: 'Choose your test' },
    { id: 'where-i-land', text: 'Where I land' },
    { id: 'question', text: 'The better question' },
];

const subjects: ReadonlyArray<SubjectResults> = [
    { name: 'HTML', path: '/HTML', results: htmlResults },
    { name: 'CSS', path: '/CSS', results: cssResults },
    { name: 'HTML and CSS', path: '/Together', results: togetherResults },
];
---
<PartLayout part={part} outline={outline}>
    <p>
        Four parts, four tests, and a lot of checkboxes later, here's where the evidence leaves us. The short version is
        that "is HTML a programming language?" has no single answer, because it's really four questions in one. The
        long version is below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scorecard">The scorecard</h2>
    <p>
        Here are the results from parts 2, 3 and 4 side by side. Each column links to the part that explains its
        scores.
    </p>

    <Scorecard subjects={subjects} />

    <p>
        Read down the columns and a pattern appears. HTML on its own passes the loosest test and little else. CSS on its
        own does better than its reputation, and is still getting better. Together, they pass the middle test outright
        and get a foot in the door of every other.
    </p>
    <p>
        Read across the rows and a different pattern appears: the answer depends far more on the test than on the
        language. Nobody disagrees about what HTML can do. They disagree about what counts.
    </p>

    <h2 id="choose">Choose your test</h2>
    <p>
        So pick the test you believe in, and see the verdict it gives. (This is HTML and CSS too: the choices are radio
        buttons, and CSS shows the matching verdict.)
    </p>

    <div class="choose surface">
        <fieldset class="choices">
            <legend class="visually-hidden">Which test makes something a programming language?</legend>
            <label><input type="radio" name="test" id="choose-compute" /> It can compute anything</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="test" id="choose-instruct" /> It gives a machine instructions</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="test" id="choose-decide" /> It can decide and remember</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="test" id="choose-programs" /> People build programs with it</label>
        </fieldset>

        <div class="verdicts" aria-live="polite">
            <p class="verdict verdict-none">Choose a test to see its verdict.</p>
            <div class="verdict verdict-compute">
                <p class="verdict-headline">HTML: no. HTML and CSS: yes, with conditions.</p>
                <p>
                    HTML can't calculate anything at all. HTML and CSS together can run Rule 110, which can compute
                    anything, as long as a person keeps clicking; and the newest CSS can run an emulated processor by
                    itself, in some browsers. By the strictest test, HTML alone is out, and the pair have only just got in.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict verdict-instruct">
                <p class="verdict-headline">Yes: HTML is a programming language, and so is CSS.</p>
                <p>
                    Every element and every rule is an instruction a browser carries out. The trouble with this test is
                    how much else passes it: a knitting pattern, a sat-nav route, the settings on a washing machine. If you
                    choose it, you've chosen a very big club.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict verdict-decide">
                <p class="verdict-headline">Neither alone. Together: yes.</p>
                <p>
                    HTML remembers without deciding; CSS, in everyday use, decides without remembering. Put them together
                    and you get choices, memory and logic, and every demo on this site is the proof. By this test, HTML on
                    its own falls short, but HTML and CSS together are a programming language, if a very limited one.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict verdict-programs">
                <p class="verdict-headline">Mostly not.</p>
                <p>
                    People build pages and applications' screens with HTML and CSS, and leave the logic to other languages.
                    The demos show games and calculators can be built from them, and x86CSS even runs compiled programs,
                    but nobody would build real software that way. By the test of everyday use, they're languages for
                    describing, not for programming.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p>
        None of the four is wrong. Each captures something real about programming. That's why the argument never ends:
        two people who pick different tests can both be right, and both be sure the other is wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where-i-land">Where I land</h2>
    <p class="byline">My own view, as the author of this site: <a href={site.authorUrl}>{site.author}</a>.</p>
    <p>
        I'm undecided, and I think that's the honest position rather than a dodge. HTML can, in some sense, be considered
        a programming language, albeit a very weak one. Professor Brailsford's view of it as a simple, declarative
        language, in which elements are something like functions telling the browser what to render, is a fair
        description of what HTML is. But HTML on its own could never crack the Enigma machine, or complete a knight's
        tour, and I can't call something a programming language when it can't do either.
    </p>
    <p>
        Where it gets interesting is when CSS steps in, and that's what the original version of this site got wrong: it
        looked almost entirely at HTML. CSS has spent the last few years gaining exactly the things programming needs:
        conditions, calculations, functions, and now, through animations, a clock and a kind of memory. HTML and CSS
        together are already a programming system, a limited and awkward one. If CSS keeps going the way it is, the
        debate may end up being settled not by an argument, but by CSS.
    </p>

    <h2 id="question">The better question</h2>
    <p>
        Much of this debate isn't really about HTML. "That's not real programming" is often a way of drawing a line
        between people, not languages, and lines like that tend to move. Alvaro Montoro put it well:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
        <p>
            The definition of what programming is (or is not) changes with time. I bet someone sorting through punched
            cards complained about how typing code in assembly was not real programming.
        </p>
        <p class="quote-source">
            <a href="https://css-tricks.com/html-is-not-a-programming-language/">Alvaro Montoro, CSS-Tricks</a>
        </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
        So the label matters less than the question it makes us ask: <strong>when do descriptions, rules and state become
        programming?</strong> HTML and CSS sit right on that line, which makes them the best place to think about it.
        Whatever you decide, you'll have decided it about the tests, not the languages, and that's the most useful thing
        this debate can teach.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you want to go further, the <a href="/Sources">sources</a> collect the articles, talks and projects behind
        everything here, and the <a href="/Demos">demos</a> are there to make up your own mind.
    </p>
</PartLayout>

<style>
    .byline {
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
        font-style: italic;
    }

    .quote-source {
        margin-top: 0.75rem;
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }

    .choose {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1.25rem;
        padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    }

    .choose > :global(*) {
        margin-top: 0;
    }

    .choices {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.5rem;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        border: 0;
    }

    .choices label {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.6rem;
        padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--heading);
        font-size: var(--text-base);
        font-weight: 500;
        cursor: pointer;
    }

    .choices label:has(:checked) {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px var(--brand-blue);
    }

    .choices input {
        width: 1.1rem;
        height: 1.1rem;
        accent-color: var(--brand-blue);
    }

    /* Each verdict waits for its test to be chosen; until one is, a prompt shows instead. */
    .verdict {
        display: none;
    }

    .verdict > :global(* + *) {
        margin-top: 0.75rem;
    }

    .verdict-none,
    .choose:has(#choose-compute:checked) .verdict-compute,
    .choose:has(#choose-instruct:checked) .verdict-instruct,
    .choose:has(#choose-decide:checked) .verdict-decide,
    .choose:has(#choose-programs:checked) .verdict-programs {
        display: block;
    }

    .choose:has(.choices :checked) .verdict-none {
        display: none;
    }

    .verdict-none {
        color: var(--text-muted);
    }

    .verdict-headline {
        color: var(--heading);
        font-size: var(--text-xl);
        font-weight: 600;
    }
</style>
